course: dictionary
lesson: 1938
title: Commentary: words that describe what is happening
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A commentary is a set of words that describes or explains something. Often it is spoken while something happens, like a football match on the radio. The speaker tells you what is going on as it goes on.
explain: «And Ruairí scores AGAIN, the crowd goes mental!» Ruairí is kicking a ball against a barn wall and talking about his own game. Words like «scores» and «the crowd» tell you it is a sports commentary, even though no crowd is there.
explain: A commentary can also be written notes that explain a book, such as a Bible commentary. It can even mean opinions about something, like «social commentary». Look at the words around it to work out which meaning fits.
fact: A commentary is a description or set of remarks about something.
fact: A live commentary is spoken while an event is happening, such as a sports match on TV or radio.
fact: A running commentary is non-stop comments on what is happening.
fact: A Bible commentary is a set of written notes that explain a book of the Bible.
fact: Commentary can also mean opinions and remarks about a topic, as in «social commentary».
exercise: Ruairí kicks a ball against the barn wall and talks through every kick. What kind of commentary is this? | A written commentary on a book | A Bible commentary | *A running commentary, with non-stop comments
exercise: «Rabbi Hillel said the rest is commentary.» In this sentence, what does commentary mean? | A goal shouted on the radio | A kind of ball game | *Extra remarks that explain the main idea
exercise: What is a Bible commentary? | *A set of written notes that explain a book | A prayer said before a meal | A football chant | A spoken description of a race on TV
test: Which phrase means a spoken description while an event is happening? | *Live commentary | Social commentary | Bible commentary
test: Which phrase means non-stop comments? | Written commentary | Bible notes | *Running commentary
exam: In «a Bible commentary», what does commentary mean? | A goal shouted on the radio | A dog barking | A kind of Bible verse | *Written notes that explain a book
